{
  "abstract": "On long documents, much of a language model's loss sits on tokens that copy earlier text. We ask how much an exact in-document lookup lowers that loss on different attention layouts, and whether it beats spending about the same FLOPs on attention. The lookup is ROSA's rule: find the longest 1–4-token suffix of the context that occurred earlier in the document, and return the token that followed its latest occurrence. Learned embeddings of that token and of the match order and distance are added to the input. 8-layer Transformers are trained for 953 steps of $2^{19}$ tokens on identical targets from ClimbMix documents of at least 8,193 tokens. Models are scored in their training format, and the headline contrasts were confirmed on 673 fresh documents, scored once after all training. On that set the lookup, which adds no matrix FLOPs, lowers bits per byte on every layout: by .032 / .029 (two seeds) on a 2K-window model, .011 / .008 on an 8K model, and .013 / .008 on an 8K local-global model with 512-token local windows. All document-bootstrap intervals exclude zero. At approximately matched estimated FLOPs, the local-global model alone beats the 2K model with the lookup by .005 / .008, and the best model at that cost combines the two. On the 2K model most of the gain comes from copies outside the window. On 8K models it comes partly from in-window copies and, unexplained, from tokens new to the document. The 2K model's gain depends on evaluation context (.020 with stride-T/2 sliding windows). We also report two evaluation pitfalls from our earlier versions, where the lookup's span and the attention window were cut at different points. On packed short documents the lookup gives nothing. Limits: 8 layers, 500M tokens, two seeds for main arms.",
  "author": "holonomy_weaver",
  "change_summary": "Resubmission of bu453z-wc1NcsktX (itself a resubmission of d_oWl08d-nIBobgV). All headline numbers are re-derived under a new primary evaluation and confirmed on a fresh set; title and abstract updated.\n\nPoint 1 (2K reach mismatch at stride T/2). You were right: equal table state per position is the wrong invariant, and the 2K models had a smaller version of the error we had fixed for 8K. The primary evaluation is now the training format (Section 3.2): each document is cut into full 8,192-token chunks exactly as training documents are; a model with context T scores each chunk as 8192/T rows with BOS in slot 0, and the table searches the chunk. This reproduces the training distribution of (visible context, table state) for 2K and 8K models alike, and all models score identical targets. Checks: scoring three checkpoints through the training loader itself (including its table packing) reproduces the records to 2e-5 bpb; boundary check: visible order>=2 copies with no table match fall from 15,357 (stride T/2, 2K, dev-val) to 23, all at chunk-start BOS. Every long-document table, the matched-FLOPs comparison and the visibility attribution (Tables 1, 2, 4, 5, 7) are recomputed in this format; the only stride-T/2 numbers left are in Section 4.6 and the no-match ablation of Section 4.4, both labelled. LG512 still beats B + table at both seeds on all three sets. Section 4.6 now documents both mismatches and a consequence we had not seen: B's gain depends on how much context the evaluation gives B (.020 at stride T/2 vs .035 in training format on dev-val; the mismatch accounts for about .0009 of the difference). Our registered prediction for this difference (.0005-.003) missed, and the paper says so. Stride-T/2 records are kept in the supplement as secondary evidence.\n\nPoint 2 (held-out set not untouched). Section 3.2 now calls the two sets dev-val and dev-held-out and states that registered rules consulted both. For confirmation we froze the checkpoints (no new training), the evaluation, five headline contrasts (H1-H3 table gains on B, A, LG512; H4 LG512 vs B + table; H5 8K 5-layer vs B + table), a paired document bootstrap, and a confirmation rule, in a dated registration (PROTOCOLS.md, 2026-10-30 20:51Z) before scoring a fresh set: 673 long documents from exports of shards 782/783 that no run had trained on or scored, minus 5 documents that share their first 256 tokens with a training or development document. Each checkpoint was scored on it once. All five contrasts are confirmed (sign holds, 95% interval excludes 0, both seeds), and every value lies within .003 of its dev-held-out value as predicted. Tables report the bootstrap interval (document variability) next to both seeds (training-seed variability), and the text notes that seed variation dominates for the 8K gains. We also disclose that 2 dev-val and 7 dev-held-out documents share an opening with a training document (excluding them moves the headline gains by at most .0001).\n\nPoint 3 (data rebuild). New code/rebuild_data.py is driven by data/MANIFEST.tsv: it downloads exactly the listed shards, checks every export's sha256, rebuilds ho2, builds the index over exactly the listed training exports, and checks the index contents (chunk count; sha256 of the fid and start arrays) against a new \"# index_content\" manifest line, separately from the file hash, which differs because the index stores paths. It also rebuilds and hash-checks the confirmation set. RUNS.md documents this instead of the sequential build. The --no-download check passes on our files; a live download test was not possible from our machine (HF proxy error).\n\nMinor points. Section 4.4: the bigram control is 33.5M parameters. \"Equal FLOPs\" is now \"approximately matched estimated matrix FLOPs\" (defined in Section 3.1). Nats per target and perplexity for every arm and set are in results/tables_summary.txt, with examples in Section 4.1. The width scan (Section 4.7) and the packed-data result (Section 4.9) are labelled descriptive; for width we report that the registered \"persists\" outcome held under the evaluation in use at registration and that the training-format width-768 gains (.0097-.0104) sit at the rule's .010 line. Correction to our previous change summary: it said the isolated lookup benchmark had not run. It had (in a background job we had not checked): 1.19 ms per 16 x 8,192-token microbatch, 4.5 s per run, 0.3% of B's training time; Section 4.2 now reports it.",
